# IQX Ixia commit-msg hook (fork-only)

This folder exists only in the personal fork cloud-practitioner/firstmate.
It is for IQX Bitbucket repositories (`bitbucket.org/iqxbusiness`) only.
It is never offered upstream and must not be part of any pull request to kunchenguid/firstmate.
Nothing in `bin/`, the skills or `AGENTS.md` calls it; the operator runs it by hand.
Upstream merges normally retain this fork-only folder; if upstream ever introduces the same path, preserve this contribution when resolving the merge.

## Why it exists

Every commit message on an IQX Bitbucket repository must end with exactly one line:

`Co-Authored-By: Ixia Engineering (Claude) <noreply@anthropic.com>`

Firstmate's commit-msg hook (`bin/fm-git-strip-ai-trailers.sh`) strips known AI attribution trailers, and that line names `noreply@anthropic.com`, so it is stripped too.
The generated hook then runs the repository's own `.git/hooks/commit-msg` on the same message file.
`ixia-commit-msg-hook.sh` is that repository hook: after the strip it re-adds the Ixia trailer, subject to the limits below.
It acts only when `remote.origin.url` names `bitbucket.org/iqxbusiness` and leaves a message that git will treat as empty alone so git still aborts.
Human trailers and all other non-Ixia lines are preserved byte-for-byte; only existing Ixia lines and trailing blank lines are removed.
The hook uses `git interpret-trailers --parse` read-only to recognize a final trailer block, then appends Ixia directly to that block or after one blank line otherwise, before discarded comment and scissors sections.

The current hook is v2, sha256 `3fa6e564eda1ffa16cdc3623df15878c59ab7cd8f9eff74b53255e112fc06685`; both scripts refuse to run if the bundled copy differs.
v2 judges the message the way git will commit it, so a subject or body line starting with `#` is handled correctly:
- It works out git's cleanup mode: the last `--cleanup` flag when git's own argument list is available (Linux and macOS), else `commit.cleanup`, else strip when an editor is used and whitespace when it is not.
  Editor use is resolved from git's arguments, including explicit `--edit` and `--no-edit`, not from comment-looking message text.
- Under `git commit -m` a `#` line is ordinary text and gets the Ixia line; under an editor or `--cleanup=strip` it is a comment, and a message of only comments is still left alone so git aborts.
- Scissors sections are excluded only when git truncates them: verbose commits, or scissors cleanup with an editor, using the configured comment delimiter. Otherwise the marker and following text are ordinary message content.
- Emptiness, deduplication, trailer placement and the final exactly-one-line check all use that same boundary; verbatim whitespace-only messages are nonempty.

v1 (sha256 `39c0d3c156f1b8ba404dcd5797874947b22c07e82ed06ff15f5f6837a19e8472`, kept in `previous/`) left a `git commit -m '#123 fix'` commit without the line.
The install script upgrades an exact v1 copy to v2.

## Files

- `ixia-commit-msg-hook.sh` - the v2 hook; installed as `commit-msg`.
- `previous/ixia-commit-msg-hook.v1.sh` - the superseded v1 hook, kept so the installer's upgrade and its test have exact v1 bytes.
- `ixia-hook-install.sh` - installs the hook into named clones of one firstmate home.
- `ixia-hook-verify.sh` - checks the installation without committing in the real clone.
- `ixia-hook-lib.sh` - helpers shared by the two scripts.
- `ixia-hook.test.sh` - lab-repository test, including byte-preservation, the `#` cases and the v1 upgrade; run `bash contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook.test.sh`.
  It lives here, not in `tests/`, so the repository's test inventory and CI do not pick it up.

## Install and verify

Both scripts take project names that exist as `projects/<name>` in a firstmate home.
Run them from that home's root; set `FORK` to the absolute path of a checkout of the fork so it still resolves after changing directories.

```bash
FORK="/absolute/path/to/cloud-practitioner/firstmate"
cd <firstmate home>
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-install.sh" <project>...
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-verify.sh" <project>...
```

Per home, with that home's IQX clones (adjust the project lists to what is cloned):

```bash
cd <iqxo home>
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-install.sh" iqx.orchestration.online
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-verify.sh" iqx.orchestration.online
cd <atc home>
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-install.sh" iqx_atc_remedy
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-verify.sh" iqx_atc_remedy
cd <fab home>
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-install.sh" iqx-fab iqx-common iqx-fab_common
bash "$FORK/contrib/iqx-ixia-hook/ixia-hook-verify.sh" iqx-fab iqx-common iqx-fab_common
```

What the install does:

- It refuses a project that is not a git clone, or whose origin is not `bitbucket.org/iqxbusiness`.
- It installs to the clone's real hooks directory as `commit-msg`, mode 755, ignoring a firstmate pane's own `core.hooksPath` override.
  Linked worktrees, including every pool slot, share that one copy.
- It refuses a clone whose configuration sets `core.hooksPath`.
- It installs when `commit-msg` is absent, treats an identical v2 copy as already installed (repairing its mode to 755 if needed), replaces a byte-identical v1 copy with v2, and refuses any other existing `commit-msg`.

For output and exit codes, see `ixia-hook-install.sh --help`.

What the verify does:

- It checks the origin, the file, mode 755 (a non-executable repository hook is skipped silently by firstmate's chain), the sha256, and that every linked worktree resolves to the same hook.
- It flags an installed v1 hook and asks for the install to be re-run.
- It then makes throwaway commits in a temporary repository with the same origin URL and a copy of the installed hook: one with human trailers, one with a `#` subject under `git commit -m`.
  Each must end with exactly one Ixia line, and the human trailer must be kept.
- It never writes to or commits in the real clone; every line must read `ok <project> ...`.

## When to run it again

- After any IQX project is added to a home, or re-cloned, or a home is re-seeded: `.git/hooks` is untracked, so a fresh clone has no hook.
- After moving a clone to another path.

## What it does not cover

The hook cannot guarantee the required line in these cases, because git skips `commit-msg` or because the hook cannot see how git was started:

- The no-mistakes pipeline's own fix commits, which it makes with `--no-verify`.
- `git commit --no-verify`, which also skips firstmate's strip.
- `git cherry-pick` and `git revert`; run `git commit --amend --no-edit` afterwards.
- An autosquash `squash!` fold, which leaves a second line mid-message; amend afterwards or add `--exec 'git commit --amend --no-edit'` to the rebase.
- Bitbucket server-side merges, which write their own commit without local hooks.
- Cleanup or editor options the hook cannot read from git's argument list (for example, git started through a wrapper that hides its arguments), when they differ from configuration or the `GIT_EDITOR` fallback for that commit.

The merged result is covered by the squash-merge message rule: the squash commit message on the IQX repositories ends with the Ixia line.
